Appendix B
Generalized Extreme Value Distribution
The definition of generalized extreme value distribution, generally referred to as GEV, embraces
a family of continuous probability distributions developed within the extreme value theory. According to this latter, the extreme values in a large sample have an approximate distribution that is
independent of the distribution of each individual variable involved. Let X1, X2, ..., Xn be independent values with a common distribution function F and Mn = max1≤i≤nXi
. Hence, according
to the extremal types theorem, if there exist sequences of σn and µn and a non-degenerate cumulative distribution function G which verify the conditions σn > 0 and P{σnMn + µn ≤ z} → G(z)
as n → ∞, then G must be a GEV distribution [2]. This can be expressed as:
G(z) =
