where the parameters µ, σ and ξ are referred to as the location, scale and shape parameters respectively. The parameter µ verifies the condition −∞ < µ < ∞ and, as its definition suggests,
defines the location or shift of the distribution: thus, in the one-dimensional case, the probability
density shifts rigidly to the right as µ increases, maintaining its exact shape. The value of the parameter σ determines the statistical dispersion of the probability distribution: the larger the σ, the
more spread out the distribution is; coherently, if σ is small the distribution be more concentrated.
Finally, rather than simply shifting (as µ does) or stretching/shrinking (as σ does) the distribution,
the parameter ξ affects its shape (see Fig.B.1). In more detail:
• For ξ = 0 the GEV reduces to the Gumbel distribution. In this case the GEV is said to be of
type I.
• For ξ > 0 the tail of the distribution decreases more slowly than the tail of the Gumbel
